// std.rt is the runtime (toolchain.md 5; D13.1, D13.2).
//
// The module does five jobs. It starts and ends the process (D11.6). It
// allocates and releases heap storage (D10.2, D10.3). It writes the runtime
// error line and the panic line (D11.4). It formats each value the print
// family writes (D11.7, D12.2). It buffers those bytes for each descriptor
// (D11.5).
//
// The module defines every entry point of toolchain.md 5.1 except the two
// float printers. std.rt_float defines those, because a compiler without
// floats cannot compile them (D18.1, D18.4). The compiler holds the list of
// entry-point names and emits calls to them (D12.2). Everything else below is
// an ordinary module's own code, and a program must not call it
// (stdlib.md 1.1).
//
// The module imports std.libc and no other module (D13.1). It writes every
// byte through libc.write. It does not use stdio. So a program prints the
// bytes D11.4 and D11.7 state, not the bytes a C library chooses.
//
// Read this paragraph before you edit a failure path. A bounds check or an
// overflow check inside a failure path calls that same failure path. fort has
// no per-function safety switch, so the second failure calls the first one
// again until the stack runs out. The process then writes nothing to stderr.
// This module has no defence against that. What keeps it out of reach is
// arithmetic, and the arithmetic has no slack. Each failure path writes
// through one of three fixed arrays. DECIMAL_MAX is 20 and 20 digits is
// exactly the longest u64. HEX_MAX is 18 and 18 is exactly 2 plus the 16
// digits of the longest pointer. BUFFER_SIZE is 8192 and buffer_append
// flushes the buffer before it copies, or writes the bytes straight to the
// descriptor when they are larger than the whole buffer.
//
// format_u64 and format_hex do not stop at the end of their array. Each one
// runs until the value reaches 0, and each one writes one byte for each digit
// from the end of the array backwards. They stay inside the array because the
// counts above match exactly: the slack is 0 bytes in both. format_i64 takes
// one byte more for a minus sign, and it has that byte because the magnitude
// of a negative i64 is 19 digits at most.
//
// So a new formatter over one of those arrays, or a wider value through an
// old one, underflows start -= 1. That underflow is an overflow check, and
// the check calls fail_overflow, which calls fail_begin, which calls
// format_u64 again. The process then dies with no message at all. Widen the
// array in the same edit that widens the value, and count the digits.
//
// A check that fires in this module is a bug in this module, and it prints
// nothing when it fires. Read the arithmetic above it, not the stack.
import std.libc;

// ---- descriptors and buffers (D11.5, toolchain.md 5.3) ----------------------

// D11.5 names these two descriptors by number. The runtime buffers stdout. It
// does not buffer stderr. It tells the two apart by the number alone.
i32 STDOUT_FD = 1;
i32 STDERR_FD = 2;

// How many bytes one descriptor holds back before a write. toolchain.md 5.3
// leaves this size to the runtime.
u64 BUFFER_SIZE = 8192;

// How many descriptors the runtime buffers at once: stdout plus this many
// others. A program that writes to more descriptors than this takes the
// oldest buffer back. buffer_for flushes that buffer and gives it to the new
// descriptor.
u64 BUFFER_POOL_SIZE = 8;

// Slot 0 always holds stdout. The pool follows it.
u64 BUFFER_COUNT = 1 + BUFFER_POOL_SIZE;

// exit keeps one byte of the status (D11.6).
i32 EXIT_STATUS_MASK = 0xFF;

u64 DECIMAL_BASE = 10;
u64 HEX_BASE = 16;

// The longest decimal text: -9223372036854775808, or 18446744073709551615.
u64 DECIMAL_MAX = 20;

// The longest pointer text: 0x and 16 hex digits.
u64 HEX_MAX = 18;

// The largest u64. alloc divides it to test a product for overflow.
u64 U64_MAX = 18446744073709551615;

// D11.7 fixes the case of a printed pointer, so these digits are lowercase.
string HEX_DIGITS = "0123456789abcdef";

// The bytes one descriptor still holds. fd is -1 while the slot is free.
// is_line_buffered holds the answer isatty gave when buffer_for took the slot
// (D11.5). len counts the pending bytes in data. len is never larger than
// BUFFER_SIZE.
struct buffer {
    i32 fd;
    bool is_line_buffered;
    u64 len;
    u8[BUFFER_SIZE] data;
}

// One row of the table print_enum reads (D3.9, D12.2). This is the fort type
// of the %fort.enum_member the compiler emits (toolchain.md 6 items 2 and
// 21). The two fields are that type's two fields, in that order.
struct enum_member {
    i32 value;
    char* name;
}

// Slot 0 holds stdout. buffer_for gives the other slots out in order. When
// every slot is taken, it takes them back in the same order.
//
// These nine buffers and the message buffer below hold 82080 bytes together.
// Every program that imports std.io or std.sys imports this module, so every
// such program carries those bytes in BSS. Until T-091 retargets the
// builtins, no program uses one of them: a print reaches the C runtime's
// buffers instead.
buffer[BUFFER_COUNT] mut buffers = {};
bool mut buffers_ready = false;
u64 mut next_eviction = 1;

// A failure path builds its line in this buffer. The line then reaches stderr
// in one write, if it fits (D11.4).
buffer mut message = {};

// The string@ args_init builds from argv at process start (D11.6). args hands
// it out as a view. It lives as long as the process does.
string mut@ own mut args_store = {};

// Answers whether fd is a terminal. A terminal makes the buffer
// line-buffered (D11.5). The runtime asks this once for each buffer, when it
// takes the slot. It never asks it for a write, so no print costs a system
// call of its own.
//
// isatty fails with ENOTTY on every descriptor that is not a terminal, and it
// writes that errno. A program reads errno through sys.errno() after a call
// of its own, so this function saves errno and writes it back
// (toolchain.md 5.3). Without the restore, the first print after a failed
// open reports ENOTTY instead of the program's own error.
//
fn bool is_interactive(i32 fd) {
    i32 mut* slot = libc.__errno_location();
    i32 saved = *slot;
    bool interactive = libc.isatty(fd) == 1;
    *slot = saved;
    return interactive;
}

fn void buffers_init() {
    if (buffers_ready) {
        return;
    }
    buffers[0].fd = STDOUT_FD;
    buffers[0].is_line_buffered = is_interactive(STDOUT_FD);
    for (u64 mut i = 1; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        buffers[i].fd = -1;
        buffers[i].is_line_buffered = false;
    }
    // The failure line is assembled whole and written by its own flush, so it
    // is never cut at a newline (D11.4).
    message.fd = STDERR_FD;
    message.is_line_buffered = false;
    buffers_ready = true;
}

// Writes every byte of bytes to fd. It asks again after a short write and
// after EINTR. On any other error it drops the bytes it has not written
// (toolchain.md 5.3).
//
// No test covers either retry, and no test here can. POSIX makes a blocking
// write to a pipe or to a file return only after it has written every byte,
// and std.libc declares no pipe, no mkfifo and no socket, so no fort program
// can make a short write. O_NONBLOCK does nothing on a regular file. The
// EINTR branch needs a signal to arrive during the write, which no fort
// program can arrange either. Both branches stay because both cases are real
// outside this harness.
fn void write_all(i32 fd, u8@ bytes) {
    u64 mut done = 0;
    while (done < bytes.len) {
        u8@ rest = bytes[done..];
        i64 n = libc.write(fd, cast(rest.ptr, void*), rest.len);
        if (n < 0) {
            if (*libc.__errno_location() == libc.EINTR) {
                continue;
            }
            return;
        }
        done += cast(n, u64);
    }
}

fn void buffer_flush(buffer mut* b) {
    if (b->len == 0) {
        return;
    }
    // This reads len and clears it before the write. A write that fails then
    // drops those bytes. It does not offer them again (toolchain.md 5.3).
    u64 len = b->len;
    b->len = 0;
    write_all(b->fd, b->data[..len]);
}

// Answers whether the bytes hold a newline. A newline ends a line for a
// line-buffered descriptor (D11.5).
fn bool has_newline(u8@ bytes) {
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < bytes.len; i++) {
        if (bytes[i] == cast('\n', u8)) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Adds the bytes to the buffer. It flushes first when they do not fit. It
// writes them straight to the descriptor when they are larger than the whole
// buffer. After a write to a line-buffered descriptor that holds a newline,
// it flushes the whole buffer, which is what C does (D11.5). Bytes that went
// straight to the descriptor are already out.
fn void buffer_append(buffer mut* b, u8@ bytes) {
    u64 n = bytes.len;
    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }
    if (n > BUFFER_SIZE - b->len) {
        buffer_flush(b);
    }
    if (n > BUFFER_SIZE) {
        write_all(b->fd, bytes);
        return;
    }
    // The flush above leaves room for n bytes. The span and the copy below
    // therefore stay inside data.
    u8 mut@ room = b->data[b->len..];
    libc.memcpy(cast(room.ptr, void*), cast(bytes.ptr, void*), n);
    b->len += n;
    if (b->is_line_buffered && has_newline(bytes)) {
        buffer_flush(b);
    }
}

// Answers with the buffer of fd, and takes a slot for fd when it has none. It
// answers null for stderr, which the runtime does not buffer, and for a
// negative fd, whose writes fail and whose bytes the runtime drops.
fn buffer mut* buffer_for(i32 fd) {
    if (fd == STDERR_FD || fd < 0) {
        return null;
    }
    buffers_init();
    u64 mut free_slot = 0;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd == fd) {
            return &buffers[i];
        }
        if (buffers[i].fd < 0 && free_slot == 0) {
            free_slot = i;
        }
    }
    // Slot 0 holds stdout, so free_slot 0 means the loop found no free slot.
    // This takes the oldest slot back.
    if (free_slot == 0) {
        free_slot = next_eviction;
        next_eviction = 1 + (next_eviction % BUFFER_POOL_SIZE);
        buffer_flush(&buffers[free_slot]);
    }
    buffers[free_slot].fd = fd;
    buffers[free_slot].is_line_buffered = is_interactive(fd);
    buffers[free_slot].len = 0;
    return &buffers[free_slot];
}

// Sends the bytes to fd through its buffer. For stderr it writes them
// straight to the descriptor.
fn void emit(i32 fd, u8@ bytes) {
    buffer mut* b = buffer_for(fd);
    if (b == null) {
        write_all(fd, bytes);
        return;
    }
    buffer_append(b, bytes);
}

// Writes the bytes of a fort string. This module writes the fixed texts of
// D11.7 and D11.4 this way.
fn void emit_str(i32 fd, string s) {
    emit(fd, cast(s, u8@));
}

// Writes the bytes of a NUL-terminated C string. An enum member name is such
// a string, and so is every file name the compiler passes
// (toolchain.md 5.1).
fn void emit_cstr(i32 fd, char* s) {
    emit(fd, cast(s[0..libc.strlen(s)], u8@));
}

// Writes out the buffer of fd. It does nothing when fd has no buffer (D11.5).
// io.flush and io.close call it (stdlib.md 3).
fn void flush(i32 fd) {
    buffers_init();
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd == fd) {
            buffer_flush(&buffers[i]);
            return;
        }
    }
}

// Writes out every buffer. exit calls it, every failure path calls it, and a
// program calls it before it writes through libc.write (D11.5, stdlib.md 3).
fn void flush_all() {
    buffers_init();
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < BUFFER_COUNT; i++) {
        if (buffers[i].fd >= 0) {
            buffer_flush(&buffers[i]);
        }
    }
}

// ---- formatting (D11.7) -----------------------------------------------------

// Writes the decimal digits of v at the end of out. It answers with the index
// of the first digit. out holds DECIMAL_MAX bytes, which is the longest u64.
// The loop writes one digit before it tests v, so 0 prints as one digit.
fn u64 format_u64(u8 mut@ out, u64 v) {
    u64 mut start = out.len;
    u64 mut rest = v;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        out[start] = cast('0', u8) + cast(rest % DECIMAL_BASE, u8);
        rest /= DECIMAL_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            return start;
        }
    }
}

// Does what format_u64 does, and writes a minus before the digits of a
// negative v. No i64 holds the magnitude of i64's minimum, so the subtraction
// below runs in u64 and wraps. D11.2 spells a subtraction that must wrap -%.
// That magnitude is 19 digits, so out keeps one byte for the minus.
fn u64 format_i64(u8 mut@ out, i64 v) {
    if (v >= 0) {
        return format_u64(out, cast(v, u64));
    }
    u64 magnitude = 0 -% cast(v, u64);
    u64 start = format_u64(out, magnitude) - 1;
    out[start] = cast('-', u8);
    return start;
}

// Writes 0x and the lowercase hex digits of v at the end of out, with no
// leading zero. It writes 0x0 for 0 (D11.7). out holds HEX_MAX bytes. It
// answers with the index of the first byte of the text.
fn u64 format_hex(u8 mut@ out, u64 v) {
    u64 mut start = out.len;
    u64 mut rest = v;
    while (true) {
        start -= 1;
        out[start] = cast(HEX_DIGITS[rest % HEX_BASE], u8);
        rest /= HEX_BASE;
        if (rest == 0) {
            start -= 1;
            out[start] = cast('x', u8);
            start -= 1;
            out[start] = cast('0', u8);
            return start;
        }
    }
}

fn void emit_u64(i32 fd, u64 v) {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_u64(out[..], v);
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

fn void emit_i64(i32 fd, i64 v) {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_i64(out[..], v);
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

fn void print_i64(i32 fd, i64 v) {
    emit_i64(fd, v);
}

fn void print_u64(i32 fd, u64 v) {
    emit_u64(fd, v);
}

// Writes true or false. D11.7 fixes those two texts.
fn void print_bool(i32 fd, bool v) {
    if (v) {
        emit_str(fd, "true");
        return;
    }
    emit_str(fd, "false");
}

// Writes the byte of c. D11.7 prints a char as its byte, whatever that byte
// is.
fn void print_char(i32 fd, char c) {
    u8[1] mut one = {};
    one[0] = cast(c, u8);
    emit(fd, one[..]);
}

// Writes 0x and lowercase hex digits. It writes 0x0 for null (D11.7).
fn void print_ptr(i32 fd, void* p) {
    u8[HEX_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_hex(out[..], cast(p, u64));
    emit(fd, out[start..]);
}

// Writes the bytes of a string (D11.7). len is the string's own length. It
// counts no terminator. The zero string has no byte to write.
fn void print_str(i32 fd, char* ptr, u64 len) {
    if (len == 0) {
        return;
    }
    emit(fd, cast(ptr[0..len], u8@));
}

// Writes the name of the first member whose value is v. It writes v itself
// when no member holds v (D11.7, D7.7). m points at the table the compiler
// emits and n counts its rows (toolchain.md 6 item 21).
fn void print_enum(i32 fd, i32 v, enum_member* m, u64 n) {
    enum_member@ table = m[0..n];
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (table[i].value == v) {
            emit_cstr(fd, table[i].name);
            return;
        }
    }
    emit_i64(fd, cast(v, i64));
}

// ---- failures (D11.4, toolchain.md 5.2) -------------------------------------

fn void message_str(string s) {
    buffer_append(&message, cast(s, u8@));
}

fn void message_cstr(char* s) {
    buffer_append(&message, cast(s[0..libc.strlen(s)], u8@));
}

fn void message_u64(u64 v) {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_u64(out[..], v);
    buffer_append(&message, out[start..]);
}

fn void message_i64(i64 v) {
    u8[DECIMAL_MAX] mut out = {};
    u64 start = format_i64(out[..], v);
    buffer_append(&message, out[start..]);
}

// Flushes every buffer. It then starts the failure line with the position and
// the kind. The kind is "runtime error", "panic" or "assertion failed". D11.4
// fixes this shape for all three.
fn void fail_begin(char* file, u32 line, u32 col, string kind) {
    flush_all();
    message.len = 0;
    message_cstr(file);
    message_str(":");
    message_u64(cast(line, u64));
    message_str(":");
    message_u64(cast(col, u64));
    message_str(": ");
    message_str(kind);
    message_str(": ");
}

// Ends the line, writes it, and calls abort. The process then ends with
// SIGABRT (D11.4).
fn noreturn fail_end() {
    message_str("\n");
    buffer_flush(&message);
    libc.abort();
}

fn noreturn fail_runtime(char* file, u32 line, u32 col, string what) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str(what);
    fail_end();
}

fn noreturn fail_bounds(i64 index, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("index ");
    message_i64(index);
    message_str(" out of range for length ");
    message_u64(len);
    fail_end();
}

fn noreturn fail_span(i64 lo, i64 hi, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("span bounds ");
    message_i64(lo);
    message_str("..");
    message_i64(hi);
    message_str(" out of range for length ");
    message_u64(len);
    fail_end();
}

fn noreturn fail_overflow(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "integer overflow");
}

fn noreturn fail_shift(i64 count, char* type_name, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("shift count ");
    message_i64(count);
    message_str(" out of range for ");
    message_cstr(type_name);
    fail_end();
}

fn noreturn fail_div_zero(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "division by zero");
}

fn noreturn fail_div_overflow(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "division overflow");
}

fn noreturn fail_alloc_count(i64 n, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("negative allocation count ");
    message_i64(n);
    fail_end();
}

// Reports an assignment to an own reference-typed lvalue that is not zero
// (D17.11). The compiler emits this call in checked builds only.
fn noreturn fail_overwrite(char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_runtime(file, line, col, "overwriting owned value");
}

// Reports a value that no clause of a switch over an enum names (D7.7). Such
// a value is reachable: a zeroed enum holds 0 whether or not 0 is a member,
// and a cast from an integer to an enum is unchecked (D3.9). D10.7 says the
// case is not undefined behavior, so the runtime reports it and names both
// the enum and the value.
fn noreturn fail_enum(i64 v, char* type_name, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "runtime error");
    message_str("enum value ");
    message_i64(v);
    message_str(" is not a member of ");
    message_cstr(type_name);
    fail_end();
}

// Reports the panic builtin (D12.2). The message is a fort string, so it
// arrives as a pointer and a length and may hold any byte, a NUL included.
// This module may take the name panic because it implements that builtin and
// never calls it (toolchain.md 5.1).
fn noreturn panic(char* ptr, u64 len, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "panic");
    if (len > 0) {
        buffer_append(&message, cast(ptr[0..len], u8@));
    }
    fail_end();
}

// Reports a failed assert. text is the source text of the assert argument,
// byte for byte (D11.4).
fn noreturn assert_fail(char* text, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    fail_begin(file, line, col, "assertion failed");
    message_cstr(text);
    fail_end();
}

// ---- allocation (D10.2, D10.3) ----------------------------------------------

// Answers with zeroed storage for count elements of elem_size bytes. It asks
// for one byte when the product is zero, so the result is never null and
// new(T, 0) is never null. A product that overflows and a failed allocation
// are both runtime errors at the position the caller passed (toolchain.md
// 5.1, 5.2). The name is alloc and not new, because new is a keyword
// (D2.4).
fn void* own alloc(u64 elem_size, u64 count, char* file, u32 line, u32 col) {
    if (elem_size != 0 && count > U64_MAX / elem_size) {
        fail_runtime(file, line, col, "allocation size overflow");
    }
    u64 mut size = elem_size * count;
    if (size == 0) {
        size = 1;
    }
    void* own p = libc.calloc(1, size);
    if (p == null) {
        fail_runtime(file, line, col, "out of memory");
    }
    return p;
}

// Releases storage that alloc answered with. A null p does nothing, as C's
// free does nothing. The name is free and not del, because a module-level
// declaration named del would shadow the universe function (D12.2, D7.9), and
// this module calls del on its own storage.
fn void free(void* own p) {
    libc.free(move(p));
}

// ---- strings (D3.7) ---------------------------------------------------------

// Answers true when the two strings have the same length and the same bytes.
// == and != on strings compare exactly that, so the zero string equals ""
// (D3.7). This function compares the bytes with libc.memcmp. It must not use
// ==, because == calls this function.
fn bool str_eq(char* a, u64 a_len, char* b, u64 b_len) {
    if (a_len != b_len) {
        return false;
    }
    if (a_len == 0) {
        // Two empty strings are equal whatever their pointers are, and memcmp
        // may not be given a null pointer even for zero bytes.
        return true;
    }
    return libc.memcmp(cast(a, void*), cast(b, void*), a_len) == 0;
}

// ---- process (D11.6, D8.6) --------------------------------------------------

// Builds the argument span from argv. The main the compiler emits calls this
// once, before fort_entry (D11.6). Each element views the argv bytes
// themselves, which are NUL-terminated, so a raw pointer reaches a span
// through the two-bound form (D6.9) and a string through strlen.
//
// This releases what an earlier call allocated. The C runtime overwrote and
// leaked instead. The release matters for more than the leak: without it the
// assignment below carries the overwrite check of D17.11, which the compiler
// emits in checked builds only, so the two build modes would differ.
//
// The release costs one thing. A span that an earlier args() handed out
// points into the storage this del frees, so a second args_init invalidates
// it. The C runtime leaked instead, and its old span stayed readable. The
// main the compiler emits calls args_init once (D11.6), so no program the
// compiler builds can reach that case; a program that calls args_init itself
// can.
//
// The allocation is an ordinary new. After T-091 a failed allocation here
// therefore names a line of std/rt.ft, where the C runtime printed
// <startup>:0:0. No decision states either form.
fn void args_init(i32 argc, char* mut* argv) {
    del(args_store);
    if (argc <= 0) {
        return;
    }
    u64 n = cast(argc, u64);
    string mut@ own a = new(string, n);
    char* mut@ view = argv[0..n];
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        char* p = view[i];
        a[i] = cast(p[0..libc.strlen(p)], string);
    }
    args_store = move(a);
}

// Answers with the span main received (D8.6, D11.6). sys.args() hands it on.
// The result is a view. The runtime keeps the storage (D17.3, stdlib.md 3).
fn string@ args() {
    return args_store;
}

// Flushes every buffer and ends the process with status & 0xFF (D11.6).
// sys.exit calls it.
fn noreturn exit(i32 status) {
    flush_all();
    libc.exit(status & EXIT_STATUS_MASK);
}

// ---- the C runtime, while both are live -------------------------------------

// The compiler still lowers every builtin to runtime/fort_rt.c (D12.2). A
// print, a new and a panic therefore use the C runtime's buffers and its
// argument span, not the ones above. std.sys and std.io reach those five C
// entry points through the declarations below. The tests under
// test/lang/run/stdlib call them beside the fort entry points above and
// compare the two runtimes.
//
// T-091 retargets the compiler. It deletes this section, the C file and those
// calls. D13.2 says std.rt declares no fort_rt_ symbol; that becomes true of
// this module then. These names are unmangled, so each one is the C name
// toolchain.md 5.1 fixed (D9.7).
extern fn void* fort_rt_args_ptr();
extern fn u64 fort_rt_args_len();
extern fn void fort_rt_flush(i32 fd);
extern fn void fort_rt_flush_all();
extern fn noreturn fort_rt_exit(i32 status);
